<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitution of Latvia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Satversme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, the Satversme, was adopted by the Constituent Assembly on 15 February 1922 and came into force in November 1922. It established a parliamentary republic with a single-chamber <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeima">Saeima</a> and a president elected by parliament. Suspended after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>'s coup of 1934 and during Soviet rule, it was fully restored in 1993, making it one of the oldest constitutions still in force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-latvia.jpg" alt="Constitution of Latvia" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latvijas_Republikas_Satversmes_publik%C4%81cija_1922._gada_30._j%C5%ABnija_%E2%80%9CVald%C4%ABbas_V%C4%93stnes%C4%AB%E2%80%9D.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Satversme was drafted by the Constitutional Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, which had 150 members, later 152, elected in April 1920. A Constitutional committee presented its draft on 20 September 1921, in two parts: the first regulated the state's institutions and the second the rights and obligations of citizens. The text was influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s Weimar Constitution and the Swiss Federal Constitution, and German was one of the working languages of the Assembly. The first part was passed on 15 February 1922.</p><p>The second received 62 votes in favour and 6 against on 5 April 1922, with 62 abstentions counted as noes, mostly because the parties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> opposed it, and so it was not adopted. A law of 20 June 1922 set the constitution to come into force at midnight on 7 November 1922.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The constitution consists of 116 articles in eight chapters, covering general provisions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeima">Saeima</a>, the President, the Cabinet, legislation, the courts, the State Audit Office and, since 1998, fundamental human rights. It declares <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> an independent democratic republic whose sovereign power is vested in the people. The Saeima has 100 members, elected for four years in general, equal and direct elections by secret ballot on proportional representation.</p><p>Executive power lies with the President and the Cabinet; the President's orders must be countersigned by the Prime Minister or the relevant minister, with two exceptions, dissolving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeima">Saeima</a> and choosing a new Prime Minister. Articles 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 77 can be amended only by national referendum. Eight amendments were made after independence was restored; among them the voting age fell from 21 to 18 in 1994, the Constitutional Court was established in 1996, and the Latvian language was given official status in 1998.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>'s coup of 15 May 1934 his cabinet gave the functions of parliament to the Cabinet of Ministers, until a new constitution could be drafted, which never happened. After the Soviet occupation of June 1940 the Latvian SSR adopted constitutions modelled on the Soviet ones, in August 1940 and April 1978.</p><p>Latvian lawyers and historians hold that the 1922 constitution remained in effect, since Ulmanis's declaration cancelled none of its parts and the People's Saeima of 1940 was elected under the Russian SFSR's constitution. On 4 May 1990 the Supreme Soviet of the Latvian SSR declared the restoration of independence and reintroduced articles 1, 2, 3 and 6; the whole constitution was reintroduced by the 5th Saeima on 6 July 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constitution of Latvia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Satversme</b>, adopted by the Constituent Assembly on 15 February 1922, created a parliamentary democracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
